  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Saemaul Undong Pilot Village Project for Zimbabwe</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Rural population of the republic of Zimbabwe accounts for more than 70% of its total population, and agriculture is one of the main industries of the country&apos;s economy. However, rural poverty is severe due to lack of agricultural infrastructure and access to markets, and low agricultural productivity. Villagers are not well organized due to lack of leadership training, experience in promoting village businesses, and overall, the community capabilities are very low. Villagers&apos; living conditions are also poor, and they rely on communal wells for drinking water, but they also face difficulties in procuring drinking water because they often dry up in the dry season or the pipes do not work. Due to drought and irregular rainfall, agricultural water is very scarce and irrigation facilities and agricultural technology is inadequate. There is also a lack of storage for agricultural products, resulting in frequent post-harvest losses. The project is based on close cooperation between the central and local governments of Zimbabwe and stakeholders such as villagers, and directly involves the village community (leaders and residents) in planning, implementing, and managing the project to promote sustainability and foster a sense of ownership. Conducting capacity- building trainings for residents and officials in charge, improving the living environment and agricultural infrastructure according to village-specific conditions, and promoting income-raising projects to strengthen community development capabilities and contribute to building governance and creating conditions for self-reliance.</narrative>
  </description>
